### Describe the Bug

A GraphQL query whose middle relation field is aliased `__proto__`, with an argument-bearing field one level deeper, causes `setDeep` to walk into the inherited `__proto__` of a plain intermediate node and assign a property onto the real global `Object.prototype`. This is an incomplete-fix follow-up to the null-prototype hardening from GHSA-gwvv-rr68-cmv6: that fix covers the nodes `setDeep` itself creates, but the GraphQL query parser hands it an intermediate node built with a plain `merge`, which still carries a real prototype for the walk to descend through.

No exploit (denial of service, authorization bypass, or data exposure) has been demonstrated, see the impact section. Filing this as ordinary hardening rather than a security release.

## Root cause

`setDeep` reuses a path segment whenever it is object-valued, without checking ownership:

```ts
// api/src/utils/set-deep.ts:27-34
if (typeof node[key] !== 'object' || node[key] === null) {
  node[key] = Object.create(null);
}
node = node[key];
// ...
node[segments[segments.length - 1]!] = value;
```

For a segment named `__proto__`, `node['__proto__']` reads the inherited accessor and returns the node's prototype. That is harmless as long as every node on the path is a null-prototype object, which is the guarantee the function's doc comment makes. But the GraphQL parser breaks that guarantee for the intermediate node:

```ts
// api/src/services/graphql/schema/parse-query.ts:102-105  (nested-alias handler)
setDeep(
  query.deep as Record<string, any>,
  path,
  merge({}, get(query.deep, parent), { _alias: { [selection.alias!.value]: selection.name.value } }),
);
```

`merge({}, ...)` produces an ordinary `Object.prototype`-chained object, so `query.deep.<parent>` is not null-prototype. When a deeper argument-bearing selection is then written:

```ts
// api/src/services/graphql/schema/parse-query.ts:158-172  (argument handler)
setDeep(query.deep as Record<string, any>, path /* e.g. "roles.__proto__.policies" */, merge(/* args */));
```

the walk hits `roles` (the plain intermediate), then `__proto__` (returns the global `Object.prototype`, which passes the `typeof === 'object'` check and is reused), then assigns the final segment onto it.

### To Reproduce


Any two-level relation chain where the grandchild accepts an argument:

```graphql
query {
  relationField {
    __proto__: nestedRelationField {
      grandchild(limit: 999999) { id }
    }
  }
}
```

After this request, `({}).grandchild` on an unrelated plain object is `{ _limit: 999999 }` instead of `undefined`, and `Object.prototype` has that as an own property. Running the real `getQuery` against the equivalent selection AST reproduces the write directly.

