### Describe the Bug

Multiple Directus endpoints return stable ETags, but a conditional GET made with Node's standards-compliant `fetch()` receives `200 OK` with the full response body instead of `304 Not Modified`, even when the `If-None-Match` value exactly matches the current ETag.

The behavior was reproduced on all of these endpoints:

- `/schema/snapshot`
- `/server/info`
- `/settings`
- `/presets`

This shows that the problem is not localized to the schema snapshot endpoint and appears to affect shared Directus HTTP freshness handling.

The same requests made through Bruno, against the same self-hosted Directus instance and Cloudflare URL, return `304` as expected.

Node Fetch follows the Fetch Standard and automatically adds these request headers when a caller manually supplies `If-None-Match` using the default cache mode:

```http
Cache-Control: no-cache
Pragma: no-cache
```

When `Cache-Control: max-age=0` is supplied explicitly, Directus returns `304` correctly for the affected endpoints. Without that workaround, Node Fetch clients download and process complete unchanged responses.

The `/server/specs/oas` endpoint also returns an ETag, but its ETag changed between otherwise identical requests in this test environment. It is therefore listed separately rather than used as evidence for the matching-ETag behavior above.

Expected behavior: A GET with a matching `If-None-Match` validator returns `304 Not Modified`, including when the request contains `Cache-Control: no-cache`.

Actual behavior: The request returns `200 OK` and the complete response body across multiple endpoints.

This exact Node Fetch and Express interaction has been reported upstream:

- https://github.com/whatwg/fetch/issues/1604
- https://github.com/nodejs/undici/issues/1930

### To Reproduce

1. Set `DIRECTUS_URL` and `DIRECTUS_TOKEN` for a self-hosted Directus instance.
2. Run this script with Node.js:

```js
const { DIRECTUS_TOKEN, DIRECTUS_URL } = process.env;
const paths = [
  "/schema/snapshot",
  "/server/info",
  "/settings",
  "/presets",
];
const headers = {
  Authorization: "Bearer " + DIRECTUS_TOKEN,
};

for (const path of paths) {
  const url = new URL(path, DIRECTUS_URL);
  const first = await fetch(url, { headers });
  const etag = first.headers.get("etag");
  await first.body?.cancel();

  const conditional = await fetch(url, {
    headers: {
      ...headers,
      "If-None-Match": etag,
    },
  });

  console.log(path, {
    status: conditional.status, // 200, expected 304
    etagMatches: conditional.headers.get("etag") === etag, // true
  });
  await conditional.body?.cancel();

  const workaround = await fetch(url, {
    headers: {
      ...headers,
      "Cache-Control": "max-age=0",
      "If-None-Match": etag,
    },
  });

  console.log(path, { workaroundStatus: workaround.status }); // 304
  await workaround.body?.cancel();
}
```

For each listed endpoint, the first conditional request returns `200` with the same ETag. Adding `Cache-Control: max-age=0` makes the same validator return `304`.

The ETag includes its complete weak-validator syntax, including the closing quote, for example:

`W/"9949c-6dUNWWg2OUQOsv1H9W8dONTcyYw"`

Environment used for reproduction:

- Directus 12.1.1
- Node.js 24.15.0
- Self-hosted using the Directus Docker image
- PostgreSQL
- Cloudflare proxy in front of Directus
- Bruno against the same public endpoints returns 304 when sending the same `If-None-Match` value without request-side `Cache-Control: no-cache`.

